Q: Is there a css equivalent of pointer-events but for keyboard events? A: <p>You asked to do it without JavaScript (which is only possible if the web browser developers do it for you), since you claim to know how but aren't sharing it to help other users. So, I've taken the liberty of doing it with the &quot;<strong><code>keydown</code></strong>&quot; event and the &quot;<strong><code>pointer-events: none</code></strong>&quot; style.</p>
<p>Here's some illustrative code showing how to do it.</p>
<p><div class="snippet" data-lang="js" data-hide="false" data-console="true" data-babel="false" data-babel-preset-react="false" data-babel-preset-ts="false">
<div class="snippet-code">
<pre class="snippet-code-js lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>var k = ["Tab","Escape","F5"];
var e = document.querySelectorAll('input[readonly][type="checkbox"],input[readonly][type="radio"],select[readonly]');
e.forEach(function(e) {
  e.addEventListener("keydown", function(e) {
    if ( ! (k.indexOf(e.key) +1) ) {
      e.preventDefault();
    }
  })
})</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-css lang-css prettyprint-override"><code>input[readonly][type='checkbox'],
input[readonly][type='radio'],
select[readonly] {
  pointer-events: none
}</code></pre>
<pre class="snippet-code-html lang-html prettyprint-override"><code>&lt;b&gt;readonly input:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;
input: &lt;input readonly&gt;
checkbox: &lt;input type="checkbox" readonly&gt;
radio: &lt;input type="radio" readonly&gt;
select:&lt;select readonly&gt;
 &lt;option&gt;11&lt;/option&gt;
 &lt;option&gt;12&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;

&lt;b&gt;standard input:&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;
input: &lt;input&gt;
checkbox: &lt;input type="checkbox"&gt;
radio: &lt;input type="radio"&gt;
select:&lt;select&gt;
 &lt;option&gt;21&lt;/option&gt;
 &lt;option&gt;22&lt;/option&gt;
&lt;/select&gt;</code></pre>
</div>
</div>
</p>

Q: Not able to validate the error tooltip as it does not show in DOM A: <p>I see a few options:</p>
<ol>
<li>Do a screenshot test to see if the tooltip is present. This is slow and heavy, but it's possible.</li>
<li>Assert on the Angular classes. When there's text added, <code>ng-invalid</code> changes to <code>ng-valid</code>. This isn't user visible, but is fast.</li>
<li>Submit the form and assert that the URL is the same. This doesn't directly validate the error tooltip, but it's worth mentioning.</li>
</ol>
<p>Here's an example of all 3:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import {expect, test } from &quot;@playwright/test&quot;; // ^1.53.0

test(&quot;test first name input validation failure&quot;, async ({page}) =&gt; {
  const url = &quot;https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html&quot;;
  await page.goto(url, {waitUntil: &quot;commit&quot;});
  const firstName = page.getByPlaceholder(&quot;First Name&quot;);
  const form = page.locator(&quot;form&quot;);
  const firstNameClass =
    &quot;form-control ng-pristine ng-untouched ng-invalid ng-invalid-required&quot;;
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(firstNameClass);
  await page.locator(&quot;#submitbtn&quot;).click();
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(firstNameClass);
  await expect(form).toHaveScreenshot();
  await expect(page).toHaveURL(url);
});

test(&quot;test first name input validation success&quot;, async ({page}) =&gt; {
  const url = &quot;https://demo.automationtesting.in/Register.html&quot;;
  await page.goto(url, {waitUntil: &quot;commit&quot;});
  const firstName = page.getByPlaceholder(&quot;First Name&quot;);
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(
    &quot;form-control ng-pristine ng-untouched ng-invalid ng-invalid-required&quot;
  );
  await firstName.fill(&quot;testing&quot;);
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(
    &quot;form-control ng-untouched ng-dirty ng-valid-parse ng-valid ng-valid-required&quot;
  );
  const form = page.locator(&quot;form&quot;);
  await page.locator(&quot;#submitbtn&quot;).click();
  await expect(firstName).toHaveClass(
    &quot;form-control ng-dirty ng-valid-parse ng-valid ng-valid-required ng-touched&quot;
  );
  await expect(form).toHaveScreenshot();
});
</code></pre>
<p>To emphasize: I don't necessarily <em>endorse</em> these approaches; just showing a few options to use at your discretion.</p>

Q: useNativewindColorScheme chaning theme not switching themes A: <p>A bit late, but make sure <code>ThemeProvider</code> is first in the tree like so:</p>
<pre><code>//_layout.tsx
...
return (
  &lt;ThemeProvider value={navTheme}&gt;
    &lt;GestureHandlerRootView className=&quot;flex-1&quot;&gt;
      &lt;BottomSheetModalProvider&gt;
        &lt;View className={cn(colorScheme === 'dark' ? 'dark' : '', 'flex-1')}&gt;

            &lt;AuthContextProvider&gt;
              &lt;RootQueryClient&gt;
                &lt;CheckoutContextProvider&gt;
                  &lt;UserOrderContextProvider&gt;
                    &lt;RootNavigator /&gt;
                    &lt;StatusBar barStyle={Platform.OS === 'ios' ? 'light-content' : 'default'} /&gt;
                  &lt;/UserOrderContextProvider&gt;
                &lt;/CheckoutContextProvider&gt;
              &lt;/RootQueryClient&gt;
            &lt;/AuthContextProvider&gt;

        &lt;/View&gt;
      &lt;/BottomSheetModalProvider&gt;
    &lt;/GestureHandlerRootView&gt;
  &lt;/ThemeProvider&gt;
);
</code></pre>
